1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:01,157
This $5 coffee

2
00:00:01,157 --> 00:00:03,226
could end up costing $40.

3
00:00:03,226 --> 00:00:04,925
Not because of the café.

4
00:00:04,925 --> 00:00:06,525
Because of your bank.

5
00:00:06,525 --> 00:00:08,625
Imagine you have $100.

6
00:00:08,625 --> 00:00:10,111
You buy coffee for $5,

7
00:00:10,111 --> 00:00:11,377
lunch for $12,

8
00:00:11,377 --> 00:00:12,956
gas for $28,

9
00:00:12,956 --> 00:00:15,094
and groceries for $90.

10
00:00:15,094 --> 00:00:16,476
That’s $35 more

11
00:00:16,476 --> 00:00:18,080
than you have.

12
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:20,123
Process those purchases in order,

13
00:00:20,123 --> 00:00:23,253
and only the last one causes an overdraft.

14
00:00:23,253 --> 00:00:24,368
But Wells Fargo used

15
00:00:24,368 --> 00:00:26,579
to do something different.

16
00:00:26,579 --> 00:00:29,476
It processed the biggest purchases first.

17
00:00:29,476 --> 00:00:31,361
Groceries: $90.

18
00:00:31,361 --> 00:00:32,443
$10 left.

19
00:00:32,443 --> 00:00:33,893
Gas? Overdraft.

20
00:00:33,893 --> 00:00:35,583
Lunch? Another.

21
00:00:35,583 --> 00:00:37,150
And that $5 coffee?

22
00:00:37,150 --> 00:00:38,193
One more.

23
00:00:38,193 --> 00:00:40,622
But THREE overdraft fees instead of one.

24
00:00:40,622 --> 00:00:42,189
At $35 per fee,

25
00:00:42,189 --> 00:00:45,148
that’s $105 in charges.

26
00:00:45,148 --> 00:00:47,356
$5 becomes $40.

27
00:00:47,356 --> 00:00:48,761
One customer in the case:

28
00:00:48,761 --> 00:00:50,618
$120 over…

29
00:00:50,618 --> 00:00:53,275
$506 in fees.

30
00:00:53,275 --> 00:00:55,121
After years of appeals,

31
00:00:55,121 --> 00:00:59,844
a $203 million restitution award was upheld.

32
00:00:59,844 --> 00:01:02,323
This was Wells Fargo’s old system.

33
00:01:02,323 --> 00:01:04,427
The coffee was still $5.

34
00:01:04,427 --> 00:01:07,560
The bank just made the order more expensive.
